\section{Lower the Edge, Not the Individual Merge}

A group of $\phi$ instructions is a simultaneous assignment selected by the edge that was taken. To remove the group, collect a parallel copy for each incoming edge: the destinations are the merge results, and the right-hand sides are the operands for that edge. A parallel copy first reads the sources in the old state, then writes every destination. The loss of single-definition names is intentional; changing which value a destination holds is not.

An edge with a unique source successor can often put its copies at the end of the source, after operand definitions and where the terminator will not read any clobbered location. If it is the destination's unique predecessor, they can instead go at the beginning of the destination. Otherwise the straightforward solution is to introduce a block on the critical edge and put the copies there. An unconditional copy placed in the original predecessor can clobber values on a different successor. Fabrice Rastello's discussion in \emph{SSA-based Compiler Design} covers this lost-copy problem, the swap problem, and approaches that isolate merge operands with fresh copies. Exceptional transfers and target instructions that define an operand during the transfer require particular care: neither arbitrary edge splitting nor execution before the terminator can always be assumed legal.

\section{A Backedge Swaps Two Values}

\begin{verbatim}
head: a = phi(entry: 1, latch: b)
      b = phi(entry: 2, latch: a)
      i = phi(entry: 0, latch: next)
      if i == n then exit else latch
latch: next = i + 1; goto head
exit:  return pair(a, b)
\end{verbatim}

With the input \texttt{n = 1}, the loop takes its backedge exactly once. At first entry \texttt{a} and \texttt{b} hold $(1,2)$. In addition to updating \texttt{i}, the latch edge requires the parallel assignment \texttt{(a, b) <- (b, a)}, so the returned pair must be $(2,1)$. Sequential \texttt{a <- b; b <- a} instead produces $(2,2)$. Using a temporary, \texttt{tmp <- a; a <- b; b <- tmp} preserves the old value until its final reader. A target swap instruction could achieve the same result if it is legal for these locations and leaves other live machine state unchanged.

For independent, nonoverlapping locations, discard identity copies, then emit any copy whose destination is not a source of pending copies. Such a destination cannot destroy a pending old value. If no copy qualifies, save an old value from a cycle into a fresh scratch location and retarget every pending use of that old value to the scratch. Continue until no copies remain. The invariant is that every pending source still names a location holding its required old value. Test a longer cycle and a fan-out such as \texttt{c <- a} in addition to the swap. Saving a source only for one consumer is not enough when several need it.

\section{Coalesce under a Location Contract}

Fresh virtual copies make a straightforward implementation but can increase move count. Coalescing assigns the same location to a source and destination when they can legally share it. A $\phi$ operand's value may still be used independently after the join, and copies around different merges may interact: do not equate all names of a merge just because some pairs look harmless. Check overlap of live ranges under the IR's copy semantics and the register class, fixed-location, and overlap constraints of the target. Merge isolation with short-lived temporaries makes the copy choices explicit before folding away safe moves.

If allocation ran while names were still in SSA form, the copies now shuffle concrete registers and spill slots. Fernando Magno Quint{\~a}o Pereira and Jens Palsberg's \emph{SSA Elimination after Register Allocation} considers this problem on machines without arbitrary memory-to-memory moves. It is not enough to assume a free register at every edge; a scratch plan must preserve unrelated live locations and obey instruction constraints. Plan shuffles with the actual locations, not just the identities of their SSA names.

\section{Validate Before Removing the Evidence}

Interpret the parallel assignment over distinct symbolic input tokens, and interpret the emitted move sequence over the same input state. Compare every destination and every location live through the edge. This catches the swap error as well as damage to unrelated state; a separate CFG check ensures the moves execute only on their intended edge. The dossier's Chapter~37, \emph{Compiler as a Constraint Solver}, offers a complementary view of allocation and move selection, but a cost-minimal move sequence still needs the same semantic check. Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-210} looks at validation across a larger pass boundary.
